{"reference":"Zechariah 7","book":"Zechariah","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Question About Fasting","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter is dated to Darius’s fourth year and belongs to material that substantially fits the early Persian-period setting of temple rebuilding. A delegation from Bethel asks whether the long-observed fifth-month mourning should continue. The answer redirects the question: rather than giving an immediate yes-or-no ruling about the ritual, it asks whether their fasting—and even their feasting—has truly been directed to God. The concern is not simply whether the practice continues, but what it means and whom it serves.","verse_refs":["Zechariah 7:1","Zechariah 7:1-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zechariah_007_9c819dc486d50e1b46dd5b13"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Justice and Compassion","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter’s response moves from ritual mourning to public conduct. True judgment, kindness, compassion, and protection for widows, orphans, resident foreigners, and the poor are named as commands the ancestors refused. Their refusal to listen is then connected in the passage to scattering and desolation. The chapter therefore presents justice and care for vulnerable people as inseparable from hearing the prophetic message.","verse_refs":["Zechariah 7:8-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zechariah_007_b623b366bf45436dc1f1f546"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}